<Doc>
    <p>Every server call is a handler: a function wrapped in <code>GET</code>,
        <code>POST</code>, <code>PATCH</code> or one of their siblings. A page imports it and calls it
        like any function. There is no route to register, no <code>fetch</code> and no client to keep in step
        with the server.</p>

    <Section title="A page reads a handler like a value">
        <Example
            id="contact-list"
            app="crm"
            title="`listContacts` \u2014 a handler file, read like a value"
            summary="The contacts page lists every contact. The list is empty until the handler answers, then fills in."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.abide': contactListSource,
                'src/contacts/listContacts.handler.ts': listContactsSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.test.ts': contactListTestSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactList.bench.ts': contactListBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactList />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench reads the list once the handler has answered, among 100 contacts and among
            1,000. Its ratio is what a read costs as the list grows: a read hands back what the
            handler answered, and copies nothing.</p>

        <p><code>listContacts.handler.ts</code> runs on the server. The list imports it like any module,
            and the browser is sent a call under the same name in its place, so there is no
            <code>fetch</code>, no route to register, and no second copy of the contact type.</p>

        <p>The test mounts the list, and the handler runs in process. A handler only one file
            calls can live in that file's <code>&lt;script server&gt;</code> instead. See
            <a href={url('/abide-files/blocks')}>Script and style blocks</a>.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A page calls a handler with typed arguments">
        <Example
            id="deal"
            app="crm"
            title={'`getDeal({ id })` — a handler called with typed arguments'}
            summary="Pick a deal. The page asks the server for that one, and its fields fill in when it lands."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/DealDetail.abide': dealDetailSource,
                'src/deals/getDeal.handler.ts': getDealSource,
                'src/deals/lib/DealDetail.test.ts': dealDetailTestSource,
                'src/deals/lib/DealDetail.bench.ts': dealDetailBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealDetail />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench picks a deal once the handler has answered it, among 100 deals and among 1,000.
            Its ratio is what a pick costs as the pipeline grows: the page holds the one deal its
            arguments name, and never the rest.</p>

        <p>The annotation <code>{'{ id: string }'}</code> is the handler's <code>Args</code>, and
            what it returns is its <code>Value</code>. <code>GET</code> builds the runtime schema from
            that annotation, so a request whose arguments do not match it is refused with 422 before the
            handler runs.</p>

        <p>In the browser the import is an <code>Rpc</code>: a call under the same name, with the
            same types, sent over http. The call hands back a reactive value rather than a promise, so
            the page reads <code>deal.name</code> by name and the probes are on the result.</p>

        <p>Reading <code>deal</code> starts the load. The page renders with a hole where each
            field goes, and the holes fill when the deal lands.</p>

        <p>The <code>description</code> is the sentence every surface shows for the handler:
            OpenAPI, MCP and the CLI. <code>refuse(404)</code> answers a deal that does not exist.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Four methods declare a write">
        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>For</th><th>Arguments travel</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>POST(fn)</code></td><td>a new deal</td><td>in the body</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>PATCH(fn)</code></td><td>a change to part of one</td><td>in the body</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>PUT(fn)</code></td><td>a deal replaced whole</td><td>in the body</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>DELETE(fn)</code></td><td>a deal removed</td><td>in the URL, flat</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>POST</code> / <code>PUT</code> / <code>PATCH</code> / <code>DELETE</code> each take
            what <code>GET</code> takes and are called the way it is. The page never names the method;
            the handler carries it. A <code>GET</code> must not write, because a link on another
            site can reach it with your user's cookie attached.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A handler can answer with a file">
        <Example
            id="export"
            app="crm"
            title="`exportDeals.url()` — a CSV file a link downloads"
            summary="Sales wants the pipeline in a spreadsheet. The link downloads every deal as a CSV file."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/ExportDeals.abide': exportDealsSource,
                'src/deals/exportDeals.handler.ts': exportDealsHandlerSource,
                'src/deals/lib/ExportDeals.test.ts': exportDealsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ExportDeals />
        </Example>

        <p>A handler that returns a <code>Blob</code> answers with the blob's own type, so this one
            is <code>text/csv</code>. A link cannot call a handler, so it takes the address:
            <code>rpc.url</code> is derived from the file's path and the mount, never typed out.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You return</th><th>The caller gets</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>response.page</code></td><td>a rendered document as <code>text/html</code>: a stored invoice as it was sent</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response.json</code></td><td>JSON whatever the caller's <code>Accept</code> says, with <code>null</code> for nothing</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response.redirect</code></td><td>a navigation to another address, at a <code>RedirectStatus</code>: 301, 302, 303, 307 or 308</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>A handler's member</th><th>What you get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.url</code></td><td>the address, with the args written in, for a link, a form or a webhook</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.method</code></td><td>the verb it was declared with</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.address</code></td><td>its route, once its file has loaded, in a test as in the app</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.raw</code></td><td>the <code>Response</code> itself, for a header or a body you read yourself</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.description</code></td><td>the sentence it was declared with</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Every answer carries headers you did not write:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Header</th><th>On</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>x-content-type-options: nosniff</code></td><td>everything, so a browser never guesses a type</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: private, no-store</code></td><td>a page, a handler's answer and any refusal, until the handler sets its own</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: private, max-age=&lt;ttl&gt;</code></td><td>a <code>GET</code> over a <code>global</code> value</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: no-store</code></td><td>the OpenAPI document and the MCP endpoint</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>cache-control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable</code></td><td>the built bundle, named by its content hash</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`refreshes` reloads every tab showing what a write changed">
        <Example
            id="refreshes"
            app="crm"
            title={"`POST(write, { refreshes: ['stage:lead'] })` — reload by tag after a write"}
            summary="The same board, with a write that says what it changed. Qualify a lead, in two tabs if you have them."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/QualifyBoard.abide': qualifyBoardSource,
                'src/pipeline/qualifyLead.handler.ts': qualifyLeadSource,
                'src/pipeline/getStage.handler.ts': getStageSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/QualifyBoard.test.ts': qualifyBoardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <QualifyBoard />
        </Example>

        <p>The button calls <code>qualifyLead()</code> and nothing after it. The handler lists the
            tags it changes, and <code>getStage</code> tags each entry with its stage, so the
            <code>lead</code> and <code>qualified</code> entries reload and the other two do not.
            The refresh runs on the server, so every tab showing those stages reloads them, not only
            the one that clicked.</p>

        <p>It runs once the write has answered and before the answer is sent, so the caller's next
            read is already the reload. A call that is refused, by the handler or by a
            <code>middleware</code> rung, refreshes nothing, and a streamed answer refreshes when
            its last chunk is read. Where the tags depend on the call, pass a function of its
            arguments: <code>{'refreshes: ({ id }) => [`deal:${id}`]'}</code>.</p>

        <p>Where what changed belongs to whoever called, name it by who they are. Each rep's
            pipeline is one entry of a keyed value, and the function runs in the call's request
            (16.101), so it may be <code>async</code> and read <code>principal</code>.
            <code>{'refreshes: async () => [[pipelines, { repId: (await principal.resolved).id }]]'}</code>
            reloads that rep's entry, as <code>{'refresh(pipelines, { repId })'}</code> would. Only
            the tabs following it are sent the new pipeline. One list can name tags, selections and
            <code>[selection, pattern]</code> pairs together.</p>

        <p>Some views belong to one browser: a draft, a cart, the deals one person has starred.
            A refresh on the server reaches every tab following the handler, so pass the browser it
            is for: <code>{'getStarred.refresh(undefined, { caller: true })'}</code> reloads the
            copies the browser making this request follows, in every tab of it, and leaves every
            other browser alone. A write says the same as a triple,
            <code>{'refreshes: [[getStarred, undefined, { caller: true }]]'}</code>, and a call no
            browser made reaches none. Only the server knows a browser's caller, so a page that
            passes one is told so.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{ caller }'}</code></td><td>the second argument to <code>rpc.refresh</code> and <code>rpc.invalidate</code> on the server: reach only the browser whose <code>principal.caller</code> it is, or <code>true</code> for the one making the request</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>refreshes</code></td><td>the tags, selections, <code>[selection, pattern]</code> pairs and <code>[rpc, pattern, {'{ caller }'}]</code> triples a write refreshes each time it answers, or a function of its arguments returning them, <code>async</code> where it reads <code>principal</code>. <code>POST</code>, <code>PUT</code>, <code>PATCH</code> and <code>DELETE</code> take it; <code>GET</code> does not, since a read changes nothing to refresh</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel on the server reaches every open tab">
        <Example
            id="live-feed"
            app="crm"
            title="`GET` + `POST` — one account's feed, every tab"
            summary="The activity feed on Hopper & Co. Open this page in a second tab, log a call in either, and both lists grow."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/AccountFeed.abide': accountFeedSource,
                'src/activity/lib/feeds.ts': feedsSource,
                'src/activity/feed.handler.ts': feedSource,
                'src/activity/logToFeed.handler.ts': logToFeedSource,
                'src/activity/lib/AccountFeed.test.ts': accountFeedTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <AccountFeed />
        </Example>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>What it is</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'reactive(fn, { global: true })'}</code></td><td>the channels, one per account, shared by every caller</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>GET(feeds)</code></td><td>its address: a tab reading it follows it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>POST(…)</code></td><td>the way in: a handler that patches a channel</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>global: true</code> puts the channels in one cache for the whole process. Leave it
            out and a value on the server belongs to one request, so every caller gets a channel of
            their own and nobody sees anybody's calls. The body takes <code>account</code>, so there
            is one channel per account, and <code>ttl</code> lets one nobody has written to for an
            hour go. <code>{"by: 'id'"}</code> tells one entry from another, and every tab's copy
            carries it, so <code>{'{#for entry of entries}'}</code> keys its rows by id with no key
            of its own.</p>

        <p>A tab reading <code>feed</code> is subscribed to it, over one web socket that every
            followed <code>GET</code> on the page shares. The button does not paint its own entry. It
            calls <code>logToFeed</code>, the handler patches the channel, and what the patch wrote goes
            to every tab following it, this one included. Each write travels as a
            <code>PatchOp</code>, so a new entry is one <code>add</code> rather than the whole
            channel.</p>

        <p>The server writes each entry: its id, its kind and its time. A caller can only pick a
            kind the server knows, so nobody forges an entry, and who may log at all is the
            <code>POST</code>'s own middleware. The call hands back the same <code>Reactive</code>
            the handler had, its <code>Produced</code> type included, so <code>entries.length</code>
            and <code>{'{#for entry of entries}'}</code> read it like any array.</p>

        <p>The channel lives in memory. A restart starts it empty, and each instance of the app holds
            its own. Activity anyone must scroll back past that belongs in your database, behind an
            ordinary handler.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A handler that yields sends each entry as it is read">
        <Example
            id="stream"
            app="crm"
            title="`async function*` — the archive, an entry at a time"
            summary="Hopper & Co's older activity, read from a slow archive. The list fills an entry at a time."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/ActivityHistory.abide': activityHistorySource,
                'src/activity/olderActivity.handler.ts': olderActivitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/ActivityHistory.test.ts': activityHistoryTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ActivityHistory />
        </Example>

        <p>A handler written as an async generator streams. Each <code>yield</code> goes out as it
            happens, one JSON value per line, and a proxy in front passes each line on rather than
            holding it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>You get</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>{'{#for entry of entries}'}</code></td><td>every entry so far, and each one after as it lands</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>entries.busy()</code></td><td>true until the stream closes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>await entries</code></td><td>every entry, at the close</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>{'for await (const entry of entries)'}</code></td><td>each entry of the run, once, ending at the close</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p><code>for await (… of s)</code> walks the run through
            <code>s[Symbol.asyncIterator]</code>: from its first entry to its close, so a reader that
            starts halfway still gets every entry. On a value that does not stream it yields the
            settled value once and ends. The test walks the run.</p>

        <p>A page rendered on the server is not held for an unfinished stream. What the stream had
            produced is inline, and the browser picks up the rest. The transcript held for that
            handoff is capped by <code>ABIDE_MAX_STREAM_BUFFER_SIZE</code>, in bytes, unbounded by
            default. Past it the browser runs the handler again.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="An export is written as its rows are read">
        <Example
            id="csv"
            app="crm"
            title="`response.bytes(values)` — a CSV written as the cursor reaches each row"
            summary="A rep takes an account's activity into a spreadsheet. The preview above the link is the file it downloads."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/CsvExport.abide': csvExportSource,
                'src/activity/exportCsv.handler.ts': exportCsvSource,
                'src/activity/lib/CsvExport.test.ts': csvExportTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <CsvExport />
        </Example>

        <p>A helper fixes how a stream is framed, and nothing a caller asks for moves it off.
            Each takes <code>Values&lt;T&gt;</code>: an array, an iterable, an async iterable or a
            <code>ReadableStream</code>.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>The handler returns</th><th>The caller gets</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>an async generator</td><td>JSON Lines, or events for a caller asking for <code>text/event-stream</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response.jsonl(values)</code></td><td>JSON Lines, whatever the caller asks for: a list sent a line at a time</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response.sse(values)</code></td><td>server-sent events, whatever the caller asks for</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>response.bytes(values)</code></td><td>the chunks as they are, <code>application/octet-stream</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A stream whose first chunk is binary is sent as bytes without the helper.
            <code>response.bytes</code> says so before there is a first chunk, so an account with no activity
            downloads an empty file rather than an empty JSON answer.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A wallboard is sent events whatever it asks for">
        <Example
            id="ticker"
            app="crm"
            title="`response.sse(values)` — events, whoever asks"
            summary="A wallboard by the sales floor reads today's deal moves with the plain request its script has always sent."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/Wallboard.abide': wallboardSource,
                'src/activity/ticker.handler.ts': tickerSource,
                'src/activity/lib/Wallboard.test.ts': wallboardTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Wallboard />
        </Example>

        <p>A wallboard script, like <code>curl</code>, asks for nothing in particular, and an
            async generator answers that caller with JSON Lines. <code>response.sse</code> fixes the answer as
            events, <code>data: </code> and a JSON value per event, so the board reads it with the
            event-stream parser it already has. An <code>EventSource</code> asks for events by name,
            and gets them from any streaming handler without the helper.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `middleware` rung refuses before the handler runs">
        <Example
            id="admins-only"
            app="crm"
            title="`middleware: [rung]` — admins only"
            summary="Exporting every contact is for admins. Export signed out, as Theo, then as Maya."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/ExportContacts.abide': exportContactsComponentSource,
                'src/account/exportContacts.handler.ts': exportContactsSource,
                'src/account/lib/adminsOnly.ts': adminsOnlySource,
                'src/account/lib/ExportContacts.test.ts': exportContactsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ExportContacts />
        </Example>

        <p>A rung is a <code>Middleware</code>: it is handed <code>next</code> and the call's
            context, and either returns <code>next()</code> or answers in the handler's place.
            Returning <code>refuse(403, …)</code> refuses, and neither the handler nor any rung after
            it runs. A rung outside it is handed the answer from <code>next()</code>, the
            <code>403</code> response the caller gets, and never the refusal itself. A throw escapes
            every rung rather than unwinding through them.</p>

        <p>The rung sits on the handler, so it runs on every call: from a browser, from another
            handler, and from a page rendering on the server. It reads arguments through
            <code>ctx.args()</code>, which parses the body on its first call. So a rung that never asks
            refuses a signed-out caller before their body is parsed or checked.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`crossOrigin` opens one handler to a partner">
        <Example
            id="partner"
            app="crm"
            title="`crossOrigin: ['https://books.example']` — one partner, named back"
            summary="The bookkeeping partner's site reads an account statement from its own pages. Ask as the partner, then as any other site."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/PartnerStatement.abide': partnerStatementSource,
                'src/account/getStatement.handler.ts': getStatementSource,
                'src/account/lib/PartnerStatement.test.ts': partnerStatementTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PartnerStatement />
        </Example>

        <p>The answer names the partner in <code>access-control-allow-origin</code>, and the
            browser hands the statement only to a page from that origin. Every other handler in the
            CRM stays same-origin, because nothing widened it.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th><code>crossOrigin</code></th><th>Who may read the answer</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>absent</td><td>the app's own pages</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>['https://books.example']</code></td><td>those origins</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>true</code></td><td>any origin, with the caller's cookies, so keep it for answers that do not depend on who asked</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>abide answers the <code>OPTIONS</code> preflight a cross-origin <code>POST</code> sends,
            outside every rung. On a mutation, <code>crossOrigin</code> replaces the same-origin check
            a <code>POST</code> otherwise gets. Every rpc answer carries <code>vary: origin</code>, so
            a cache keeps one copy per asking origin. Pages carry
            <code>referrer-policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin</code>, so a link off the CRM does
            not hand another site the path a member was on.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`timeout` bounds how long a call may go without progress">
        <Example
            id="timeout"
            app="crm"
            title="`timeout` — milliseconds without progress, not since the first byte"
            summary="Sales by region, a row every 300 ms, under a 500 ms timeout. North runs longer than the timeout and finishes; south stops producing after its first row."
            files={{
                'src/account/lib/SalesReport.abide': salesReportComponentSource,
                'src/account/salesReport.handler.ts': salesReportSource,
                'src/account/lib/SalesReport.test.ts': salesReportTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <SalesReport />
        </Example>

        <p>On a handler that yields, the bound is per row. So one number lets a long report run
            and still catches a source that has stopped. A call past it is refused
            <code>504</code>, and the rows already sent stay on the page.</p>

        <p>Without one, nothing is cut short: the server never closes a connection under a call it
            is still answering, a slow handler or a quiet stream alike. Its sibling is
            <code>maxBodySize</code>: the bytes a mutation's body may carry, refused <code>413</code>
            before a declared oversize body is read.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th><th>What it bounds</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_RPC_TIMEOUT</code></td><td>none</td><td>the <code>timeout</code> of a handler that names none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_REQUEST_BODY_SIZE</code></td><td>none</td><td>the <code>maxBodySize</code> of a handler that names none</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_SOCKET_MESSAGE_SIZE</code></td><td>1 MiB</td><td>one message a browser sends over the socket</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ABIDE_MAX_SOCKET_SUBSCRIPTIONS</code></td><td>1024</td><td>the values one connection may follow at once</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>None of these is a rate limit. A caller sending many small, fast requests passes every
            ceiling, and a rung keyed on <code>principal.caller</code> is where a rate limit goes.</p>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
